      "claim": "Biological decomposition of organic matter is a rapid process that directly generates the organic component of soil.",
      "evidence": "Microbial and fungal activity breaks down wood into humus over months to years, which is orders of magnitude faster than the geological timescales required for significant mineral weathering.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "A rotting log in a forest provides the ideal conditions (moisture, temperature, decomposer community) for this rapid biological soil formation.",
      "evidence": "Forest floors are hotspots for decomposition where fungi, bacteria, and insects actively convert wood into nutrient-rich soil components.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
